Quoting  John H. <JohnWH@gmail.com>:
>I have a new @ Play column up at GameSetWatch:
>http://www.gamesetwatch.com/2006/09/_play_the_first_two_roguelikes.php

The assertion that AD&D 1e's random dungeon is the first roguelike is a
bit dubious. Obviously what constitutes a roguelike is a bit of a grey
area, but one of what seems like the most important characteristics to me
is that the movement and tactical combat interfaces are unified. Clearly a
DM could use the random dungeon rules in a way that encouraged
simultaneous exploration and formalised movement on the map in combat, but
generally they didn't.
